WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Let’s talk about the new race for space.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.240
Countries and companies are competing

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.600
to develop brand new space technology.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.880
Right now, engineers are designing space colonies.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.760
It’s time to go back to the moon. This time, to stay.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.280
France and the US have even launched their own space force.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.800
A space force 

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.920
as the sixth branch of the armed forces.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.320
That’s a big statement.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.640
So in this race, who could win and what’s the big prize?

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.480
And why should you and I care about what’s happening up there?

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.840
Let’s take a second to realise how close the future actually is. 

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.640
Space tourism is taking off.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.000
Billionaire Richard Branson just made his commercial space airline publicly-traded company of its kind

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.080
Virgin Galactic the first publicly-traded company of its kind.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.960
Our little company is going to be floating in space next year

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.520
and today it's floating on the stock market.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.360
And earlier this year, he opened a spaceport in New Mexico

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.680
where customers who’ve paid 250,000 dollars each

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.320
will soon board space-planes to Earth’s sub-orbit.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.080
You’ve probably also heard of the billionaire Elon Musk

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.240
and his company SpaceX.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.080
He’s unveiled detailed plans to start construction on Mars   

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.080
in just 30 years time.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.160
We want to just keep improving rocket technology

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.120
until there’s a city on Mars.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.960
Now I know it all sounds incredibly ambitious. 

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.840
But big ideas can become very real, very quickly.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.680
It’s happened before, during the first space race.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.520
In the 1950s, the US and Soviet Union 

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.720
were locked in a Cold War over political ideology.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.600
“President Truman signs a bill to spend  

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.920
over 3 billion dollars to fight communism”. 

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.680
Both sides started developing bigger and better rocket technology

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.000
to use in a potential nuclear war and to keep each other in check.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.080
But they quickly took that fight to space.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.840
“Space travel became headline news with the announcement 

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.560
that the United States plan to launch their first satellite by 1957”. 

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.800
But the Soviets beat them to it by launching Sputnik

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.120
a tiny satellite that Washington feared  

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.920
could zip over the US and gather intelligence.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.400
Four years later, the Soviet Union made history again 

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.760
by putting the first human in space.
&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.160
But soon enough, US President John F. Kennedy raised the stakes.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.280
We choose to go to the moon in this decade and do the other things.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.840
Not because they are easy, but because they are hard.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.640
In 1969, NASA put the first humans on the moon.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:41.000
That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.400
As to who won that, I think it's pretty clear that the USA won that race. 

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.840
But it wasn't just about national prestige in a simple flag-waving sense.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.040
That's why there was such an emphasis on the various scientific instruments

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.240
that were flown with the Apollo missions.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.920
What followed that first space race were, in fact, many 

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.880
decades of science-driven space exploration. 

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.080
Countries even collaborated with each other’s space programs

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.720
to send space stations, telescopes, probes  

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.200
and satellites across our solar system.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.680
NASA stayed in the lead, partly because of its space shuttle. 

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.800
But over time, governments around the world 

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.280
started pouring less money into their space programs. Including the US. 

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.760
Eventually, NASA retired the shuttle for good in 2011.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.840
And in the meantime, new space powers emerged.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.520
Countries like China and India, who have 

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.600
been pouring millions of dollars into their space programs.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.920
China and India are basically trying to one up each other in terms of getting

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.600
into record books, with China going to the far side of the moon and India

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:54.280
trying to land rovers on other planets. Which show a country's technological 

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.360
prowess, which turns into geostrategic influence.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.920
But it’s private companies, and the pockets of billionaires

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.000
who’ve really been heating up the race.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.680
SpaceX has built the world’s first reusable rocket.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.960
And the company has been working with NASA to transport cargo

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.360
to the International Space Station.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.920
The airplane manufacturer Boeing and a company called Blue Origin

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:24.720
owned by Amazon’s CEO Jeff Bezos, are also competing for contracts.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.640
So are dozens of other start-ups around the world, including 

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.600
RocketLab in New Zealand and iSpace in Japan.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.880
And the idea is that if companies can profit, science does too.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:42.240
The big prize obviously for any private entity is to be profitable and make a 

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.640
business. Space is very hard. It's very expensive. For us, for example, it's 

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.000
doing really important things that really benefit

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.880
humanity in some ways and make a profit.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.720
And the rewards could potentially be enormous.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.600
NASA believes there are nearly 800,000 asteroids in our solar system which

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.920
contain valuable metals like gold, iron and platinum.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:10.240
And just one of those asteroids, called 16 Psyche, is thought to have about 700

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.120
quintillion US dollars worth of valuable materials.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.320
There are also very real plans to mine the moon for water and Helium-3.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.760
Which could both potentially be transformed into rocket fuel.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.920
It allows us to resupply our missions to Mars

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.880
and we can even refuel orbiting satellites of Earth.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.360
And, guess what? Laws have already been drafted in the US and Luxembourg 

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.160
 to allow companies to keep what they mine

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.440
on the moon, Mars or anywhere else.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.000
Companies aren't going to invest their money if they think that whatever they

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.440
are able to mine belongs to mankind. They want it to belong to them.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.280
And the United States passed a legislation which basically said, "Okay

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.080
we're good with that". And there was some international criticism of

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:58.360
“why does it belong to you?

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.040
And that leads us to another prize in this race, which is power.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.840
Even after the Cold War, space exploration has  

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.280
in some ways been driven by the military. 

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.640
Now, government officials, military officials are saying that they would like

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.760
 to have weapons developed and deployed by 2023.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.920
SpaceCom will defend America’s vital interests in space

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.240
the next war-fighting domain.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.800
But there are no actual weapons in space. Yet.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.400
There is an internationally agreed “Outer Space Treaty” to stop weapons

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.880
of mass destruction from being used in space. 

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.880
We have very few guidelines. There are no weapons of mass destruction to be

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.200
placed in outer space, we are not to militarise the moon.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.160
But other than that, it's open territory. 

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.680
Already, the US, Russia, China and India have carried out what they call

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.560
“anti-satellite missile tests” by destroying old satellites in space.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.680
For now though, countries and companies say

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.920
they are putting science first. And that’s a good thing

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.760
because it’ll push the boundaries of human technology and could bring us

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.880
 the answers to life’s big questions.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.600
And unlike the last space race, this time there seems to be more room

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.960
for everyone. Hopefully that means everybody wins.

